\begin{abstract}
Brain tumor segmentation is a challenging and critical task in the area of medical imaging and treatment planning. The result of the segmentation process is to generate accurate sketch of the tumor regions by their corresponding brain tumor masks. The precise and accurate segmentation of tumors require multiple image modalities with varying contrasts and spatial resolution. In recent years many of the deep learning and machine learning models have emerged and achieved promising results with exceptional breakthroughs. This survery has been utilized to review state-of-the-art deep learning methods for brain tumor segmentation, clearly focusing on their approach and various strategies.This survey has also discussed on the variety of challenges faced in the process of segmentation and also have given a clear pathway with several future directions in the area of brain tumor segmentation.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Brain Tumor Segmentation, Deep Learning, Medical Imaging, MRI, Artificial Intelligence
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Brain tumor segmentation is a procedure to identify brain tumor lesions using various algorithms in machine learning. Manual segmentation of brain-scanned images is time intensive and tends to vary. Recent progress in diagnostic imaging methods have opened the door for a definite number of specialized medical practices, these techniques include computer-aided diagnosis of diseases, surgical planning and guiding, and extended observation. These brain tumor growths can be detected in no time and safely by implementing automated brain tumor segmentation by radiological imaging techniques such as computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and others. By implementing multi-modal imaging techniques such as combining MRI and CT Scans, there can achieved exceptional results in brain tumor diagnosis\cite{b6}\cite{b8}.

Deep learning models specifically those using convolutional neural networks provide a more faster and automated solution for segmenting brain tumors in  brain scan images. These models learn complex patterns and utilize data augmentation from large datasets to achieve exceptional precision and reliability in early treatment planning and tumor characterization. While fully precise segmentation remains unsolved due to several factors such as spatial uncertainty, shape ambiguity, low contrast imaging, annotation bias, and imbalanced data set. Eventually, incorporating deep learning in brain tumor segmentation is expected to result in better outcomes than manual segmentation by providing personalized, data-driven medical treatments. By leveraging segmentation techniques, tumor boundaries can be identified promptly and accurately through brain scans. There can be several factors influencing prognosis in brain tumors, by having more than 120 types of brain tumors some of them can be benign that are non cancerous and others malignant being cancerous tumors which significantly affect survival rates of the patient. The grade of a tumor reflects cellular differentiation of that type of the brain tumor, high-grade tumors (fast growing) are much likely to reappear after treatment than low-grade (slow growing) tumors. Age, Health, and Fitness will also affect the patient's prognosis, a healthy and fit patient younger than 40 are likely to recover faster from brain tumor treatment. As a result, segmented masks of brain tumors can help in the role of prognosis by determining appropriate medical treatments and early detection of the tumor by improving the survival chances of the patient. To validate the robustness and reliability of the model in terms of brain tumor prediction, cross-validation was applied. The dataset was split into multiple folds, where the model was trained on all except one fold and validated on the remaining fold. This avoids overfitting and yields a reliable estimate of the model to generalize, which is vital for accurate prognosis predictions\cite{b3}.

The current study addresses the following objectives which is to provide a comprehensive review, identify challenges and highlight research gaps in the field of brain tumor segmentation. There have been several advancements in deep learning techniques, neural networks like CNNs, ANNs and Residual Networks (ResNets) have significantly improved the accuracy of brain tumor segmentation by providing nearly accurate segmented masks of the brain scanned images. Clustering methods such as K-Means proved effective in grouping similar features of pixels from certain images for delineating tumor regions with high accuracy\cite{b2}. By also integrating Multi-Modal imaging just as in MRI modalities (T1, T2, and FLAIR) improves differentiation between healthy tissues and tumor tissues in the brain. There are still several challenges faced during automated brain tumor segmentation, Limited availability of labeled medical imaging data and inconsistency in image quality impacts the accuracy of the segmented mask, Training and Deploying dedicated machine learning models for the image segmentation require extensive computational resources. Despite the progress in brain tumor segmentation, several important gaps remain in achieving integration with clinical workflows which improves usability and efficiency and also developing a real-time segmentation solution can enhance decision making during surgical procedures. Another limitation of many models being the explainability of the inference by system and this has been addressed using SHAP\cite{b4}.

This survey states diverse advancements in the automation of brain tumor segmentation by reviewing recently developed deep-learning, machine learning and conventional based techniques. It examines strengths and limitations of different approaches, pre-processing techniques, feature extraction, diverse datasets and evaluation metrics of the models. It also discusses about the various challenges faced during segmentation and suggests a clear direction for future improvements.

\section{Background and Imaging Modalities}

\subsection{Medical Imaging Modalities}

Imaging techniques are crucial to both diagnosing and treating patients with brain tumors as they provide critical information about the tumor as well as its effect on the surrounding brain regions. Out of all the modalities, MRI is regarded as the most reliable as it captures good images of any mass and surrounding tissues using sequences T1, T2, FLAIR and contrast enhanced scans.

CT scans are also used with MRI to aid in the rapid detection of calcification or bleeding as would be seen in the processes of a PET scans, which will further assist to give more information on the metabolic activity of the tumor, and its level of aggressiveness. More complex modalities such as DTI expand the boundaries of technological advancement by supplying a detailed map of the brain’s white matter tracts, hence assisting surgeons in identifying which areas to avoid.

Taken together, imaging techniques comprise tools that deepen our understanding of the physiology and biology of brain tumors, helping to develop improved segmentation methods and more individualized treatments.


\subsection{The Role of Brain Tumor Segmentation}
Precise tumor detection is necessary for identifying tumor boundaries which in turn helps in diagnosis, planning treatment and monitoring. It helps differentiating tumors which is surrounded by non tumor tissues, ensuring accurate classification and track tumor's progress or how is the response to treatment over time.

In surgical procedures, segmentation helps highlighting the tumor's locations and boundaries of it, which enable surgeons to maximize removal of tumor while keeping the damage to vital brain structures responsible for essential functions like speech, memory and motor skills. When it comes to radiotherapy, it is very essential to make sure that radiations are targeted at the tumor, which reduces exposure to healthy tissues and minimizing side effects.
When it comes to Automated segmentation tools which brings lot of advancements to clinical by making workflows faster, more consistent diagnoses and reducing the dependency on time-consuming manual processes. These tools will be able analyze complex imaging data with higher precision and helps streamlining the preparation of personalized treatment plans. This level of automation supports personalizing medicine which helps treating patients based on their unique characteristic of patient's tumor which indeed improves outcomes.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics and Benchmarks}
The evaluation of  tumor segmentation methods involves using a set of key metrics that measure accuracy, precision and reliability in identifying tumors across diverse datasets, some of the widely recognized evaluation metrics for tumor segmentation are:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dice Similarity Coefficient (DSC):} It evaluates the degree of overlap between the predicted tumor segmentation and the actual tumor, with higher values indicating better accuracy.
    \[
    \text{DSC} = \frac{2 |A \cap B|}{|A| + |B|}
    \]
    The Dice Similarity Coefficient is a measure of overlap between two sets\cite{b11}.

    \item \textbf{Hausdorff Distance (HD):} Identifies the largest boundary gap between the predicted and the ground truth segmentations, highlighting in capturing the tumor’s edges.
    \[
    \text{HD}(A, B) = \max \left( \sup_{a \in A} \inf_{b \in B} d(a, b), \sup_{b \in B} \inf_{a \in A} d(a, b) \right)
    \]
    This equation defines the Hausdorff Distance, a measure of the maximum mismatch between two sets, as described in \cite{b12}.

    \item \textbf{Precision and Recall:} Precision shows how accurate the predicted tumor pixels are among all pixels that were predicted as tumor, while the recall measures the proportion of correctly predicted pixels of tumor among all actual tumor pixels.

    \item \textbf{Sensitivity and Specificity:} Sensitivity reflects the ability to correctly identify tumor pixels from all actual tumor pixels, and specificity focuses on correctly identifying non-tumor pixels from all the actual non-tumor pixels.
\end{itemize}
For the evaluation of these methods the survey uses publicly available datasets which provides a solid foundation. The BraTS dataset (Brain Tumor Segmentation Challenge) offers multi-modal MRI data for tumor segmentation. TCIA (The Cancer Imaging Archive) provides diverse imaging datasets, including tumor cases and ISLES focuses on ischemic stroke lesions, which share similarities with brain tumor studies. These reliable datasets paired with consistent evaluation metrics accelerates progress in the field of brain tumor segmentation.

