\section{Challenges and Research Gaps}
\subsection{Data-related Challenges}\label{AA}
Brain tumor detection studies face several hurdles related to dataset which impacts the performance and generalizability of the models. A common issue that can be observed is the limited size of publicly available MRI datasets, which can reduced accuracy on unseen data and can lead to over-fitting of data. Along this, class  imbalance within these datasets leans predictions toward the majority classes which makes it harder to identify less common tumor types. In the conference paper by Diponkor Bala et al.\cite{b3} the datasets used are heavily biased to the positive tumor examples, perhaps limiting the model's ability to generalize effectively in the real world having balanced scenarios. Quality of data is also another hurdle, as MRI scans usually contain noise, low contrast, and artifacts which requires pre-processing the data extensively to enhance image clarity. Many datasets also lack diversity as they only focus solely on single type of dataset i.e., MRI dataset and limiting model's ability to generalize across other imaging modalities like CT or PET scans. Annotating medical images is a very time-consuming process that requires experienced radiologists, resulting in fewer labeled samples for training. And also, models which are trained on single dataset usually struggle to perform well on others due to differences in imaging protocols . Lastly, ethical constraints and privacy regulations restrict access to large clinical datasets, which poses additional barriers to build robust models which will be ready for use in the real world. Addressing these issues is very essential for creating effective and reliable solutions in the detection of brain tumors.

\subsection{Tumor-specific Challenges}
The models of deep learning which enable an auto segmentation of tumors have a hard time about defining the unique characteristics that each has. It has been challenging for the optimal building of algorithms based on the dealing with variable-size tumor. the location cannot pin point more than that of the processing. for this reason the brain tumor identification is still very much not adopted.The analysis of a brain tumor is also time consuming because it determines the precise dimension during the early stages\cite{b8}. It still has many dependencies like magnetic resonance imaging(MRI), cerebral angiography, spinal tap biopsy, and other such methods\cite{b8}. The detection and characterization of tumor infected areas is possible but not accurate enough to use in real world\cite{b8}. The model has lesser chances of error than human error in detecting a tumor. Deep Learning assists sophisticated vision algorithms when analyzing medical images; however, the training process for such a large model is massive and extensive.

The models of deep learning often fail to recognize very minute, yet crucial distinguishing features that distinguish the several types of tumours. On account of the same, the generalization becomes not very apt across different sizes, shapes and locations of tumour that makes rather challenging to finally obtain optimum algorithm. And the current model doesn't have any capability to fix the exact place from where the actual tumor is appearing. As of now, hence, the perfect automation of detecting a brain tumor is just an unachieved dream. Again, the analytic process is highly labor-bound and time-taking. For humankind as well as for a machine, determining the accurate dimensions and nature at what period of its cycle the tumor appears is very a time-consuming phenomenon.

However, the techniques for the identification and classification of brain tumors are still largely dependent on various diagnostic tools and techniques. These include MRI, physical examination, and other sophisticated procedures like cerebral angiography and spinal tap biopsies. While these diagnostic procedures can be informative, they are not entirely substitute for the totality of human judgment, especially when the tumor is small or in its incipient stage. Though current models perform much better at error minimization compared to human-based approaches, real-world application demands more accuracy and automation. Thus, it becomes a challenging issue.

Deep learning, due to its ability to analyze images and identify even complex shapes and boundaries, may have applications in the medical field to enhance the detection of tumors in medical images. However, the training of such large-scale models to process and analyze these images precisely demands a lot of massive datasets, and this becomes resource-intensive and time-consuming. With all this promise that deep learning holds, challenges still lie in the availability of data, model robustness, and applicability in real-world settings, and therefore, it is not yet possible to completely rely on such models in clinical settings.
\subsection{Algorithmic Challenges}
When detecting tumors there are many factors that affect the model’s performance and accuracy. One of it is data imbalance where small tumors often get missed because of its shape and location. This imbalance makes the models focus more on the non-tumor areas and hence lower detection rates for such tumors.

Another is the high variability of tumor characteristics. Tumors differ greatly in size, shape, location and intensity across patients which makes developing algorithms that generalize well a tough task. These variations makes the models struggle to detect tumors in different scenarios.

Edge detection and boundary refinement are two of the biggest challenges. The boundaries are often unclear especially on the regions where the tumor meets the healthy tissue. Accurately outlining these boundaries is key to segmentation.

The complexity of the problem PET also scans rises is when involved. multi-modality This imaging is such because as each integrating modality MRI, offers CT different and information  about the tumor and how to  properly combine these diverse sources of information so as not to lose important information that would be helpful in segmentation.

Computational complexity is also a challenge in the process of brain tumor segmentation. Deep learning models are powerful but  computationally expensive, most especially when working with large medical images. Real time model performance without sacrificing accuracy is  an area of interest in this research.

Another problem that is quite common is overfitting and  generalization. Deep learning models can be too specific to the training data and as such may not perform well  on new data. High accuracy has been reported by Diponkor Bala et al.  [1]. This is  especially important when working with limited training datasets to avoid overfitting.

Last of all, the sensitivity  of the evaluation metrics can make way for the assessment of model performance. Different metrics such as sensitivity,  specificity and Dice Similarity Coefficient (DSC) can yield different results based on the data and  algorithm used and thus require proper tuning and validation to ensure that the models are efficient across various evaluation metrics.

These are the challenges of tumor segmentation in medical imaging, which requires further research and development.

\section{Emerging Gaps}
\subsubsection{Limited Data and Bias} The biggest limitation of any machine learning model in medical imaging, is that there is not enough good, heterogeneous data available for training a predictive model. There is a lack generalization, especially in real-life deployment. For example, almost all models can train on MRI scans for a very narrow subset of detecting tumors in the brain. This reduces its generalization abilities in the new unseen data because of the bias of some over-representation to certain tumor types, and this might also not do so well toward real-time usage. Thus, at critical times such as in patient care, a system such as this has very little pay-off since it badly loses on performance-cost balance.

\subsubsection{Transparency and Trust} Machine learning models, deep learning-based models, are often referred to as "black boxes". In medical applications, models usually pre-trained in historical data will be applied giving a prediction or classification, say tumor detection. However, such models work with no explicit reasoning for how it arrived at such a decision. This transparency makes it tough for the end-users to understand why that particular diagnosis was recommended by the system. Where the decisions might alter the life of people in a field, there is a requirement for transparency. Without it, stakeholders would not like this technology with full surrender. Such a trust deficit might limit their deployment seriously in real-world settings. If the users do not know the reliability of the system or how it works, they might prefer relying on traditional methods that they are more familiar with.

\subsubsection{Accuracy and Human Trust} The model is very impressive about what it learned from the big data, but it lacks subtlety from the human expert's information and decision in making, say, a medical imaging decision. Doctors, in that case, would rely on technical data but also years of experience, intuition, and full understanding of the patient's context.". The performance gap makes health care professionals treat the AI tools as supportive aids than as decision makers, though at present AI models are not shown to surpass human doctors consistently in every area, albeit having demonstrated very high accuracy under controlled environments. Therefore, while AI models can be useful to aid in image segmentation or so that an ancillary opinion may be given, it cannot take the entire worth of the human experience in complex cases. Thus, such a gap curtails the full potential AI may be able to contribute towards medicine as, models are thought to be of supplementary use and not a resource that is fundamentally crucial.


\section{Future Directions and Discussions}
The future of detecting brain tumors with ML and DL is very promising, but still, there are many areas that can be improved. As the technology and the available data are developed, so do the opportunities for more accurate and efficient detection of tumors.

The most promising direction this work may take in the future is through multi-modal imaging. MRI would then be supplemented by a combination of other techniques, such as CT or PET scans, for imaging in order to paint a better picture of the tumor. During so, it may exploit greater diversity of information in order to augment the MRI detector when single-modal imaging is lacking.

Another area for progress is in the development of explainable deep learning models. A model cannot just be accurate but must also be understood in medical applications. A healthcare professional will not be able to trust or use an AI system effectively without knowing how the system is making its decisions. Techniques, like SHAP or Grad-CAM may then be used with more futuristic models and better interpretations of what decisions have actually led the predictors so clinicians get to interpret more correctly. Second, it introduces the ability from transformer models successfully deployed within certain domains such as NLP; these new developments can benefit tumour detection capability as well. A very excellent ability of extracting long-range dependencies of data has been shown in transformers, and it may be particularly useful in analyzing the complex shape and nature of tumors through MRI scans. Having an immense amount of available datasets, transformers are likely to lead to improved accuracy in segmentation and the result in tumor boundary delineation.

The other challenge of \textbf{data scarcity} is an important one and continues to pose a challenge in the development of more robust AI models. The quality of data that this study has at present is okay, but still, a large number of diverse images covering most tumor types as well as diverse demographic factors can help the field move forward significantly. Larger, more diverse datasets and data augmentation strategies would help train models generalized and less likely biased by limited data.

Finally, as much success in deep learning as has been documented in the literature, real-world clinical implementation challenges remain. As such, next steps would have to include engineering AI tools as practical and deliverable tools into everyday clinical practices, providing not only speed of results but accuracy in the complex healthcare setting in which these occur. That said, integration into standard workflows, potential scalability, among other things are critical factors at this stage.

The future of detecting brain tumors by AI seems promising but depends further on the innovations in areas of integrating various sources of data, enhancement of interpretability in the model, improved training datasets, and clinical practicality. For all these fields, it continues to work in its direction towards more reliable and accessible solutions to diagnose and treat braintumors.

\section{Conclusion}
Implementing different Deep Learning (DL) and Machine Learning (ML) approaches to brain tumor segmentation is a complex challenge. However the automation of segmentation process brings in significant advantages such as reliable extraction of features and the ability to process large volumes of data quickly, which can handle complex patterns and variations in medical imaging data.
This review focuses on some effective methods where the ML and DL models are changing with the scenario in detecting brain tumors from MRI imaging data in this paper. Thus, through the analysis of various techniques that exist in machine learning models such as Convolutional Neural Networks, Artificial Neural Networks, and the traditional models used such as Support Vector Machines; This study gives a clear illustration of how the mentioned technologies help in improving precision and efficiency within brain tumor segmentation and classification. This study elaborates the key steps of pre-processing, segmentation, and feature extraction, all of which determine the success of these models. Indeed, a lot has been achieved in terms of brain tumor segmentation, but much has still to be discovered, especially in the area of the rather large volumes of diversified datasets for proper validation as well as the heavy computational powers needed for deep learning models. In addition, the complexity and lack of transparency in some of the models prevent their use in clinical practice. Future research should focus on making these algorithms more efficient, expanding the diversity of datasets, and enhancing model interpretability. In addition, by analyzing advanced techniques such as integration of multi-modal data from multiple imaging sources (MRI, CT, and PET scans) would provide a comprehensive view of the brain tumor that leads to higher accuracy in segmentation. With these improvements, AI-powered systems could become an essential tool in providing earlier, more precise and accurate diagnoses of brain tumor, which would eventually lead to better outcomes for patients.
